<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Aba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1044">1044</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Aba Sámuel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Aba was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1041 to 1044. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> of the Aba kindred and brother-in-law of Stephen I, he was raised to the throne when the nobles expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-orseolo">Peter Orseolo</a>. His rule provoked opposition among the nobles and the intervention of Emperor Henry III, who defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-menfo">Battle of Ménfő</a> in 1044; he was captured and killed soon afterwards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-aba.jpg" alt="Samuel Aba" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Márk Kálti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%A9pes_kr%C3%B3nika_-_48.oldal_-_Aba_S%C3%A1muel_kir%C3%A1ly.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
